! C2H7O3Cl | INTERMEDIATE BASIS TEST -- def2-SVP
!
! ============================================================
! Why this file exists
! ============================================================
! caa005bIRCCML1st-prerelax-631Gdp.inp confirmed a genuine minimum at
! wB97X-D/6-31G(d,p)/PCM(water): EXECUTION TERMINATED NORMALLY, and the
! final Hessian's vibrational analysis showed ZERO IMAGINARY FREQUENCIES.
!
! caa005bIRCCML1stdef2TZVPPe.inp then took that CONFIRMED-GOOD geometry and
! simply switched the basis to def2-TZVPP (same functional, same PCM
! treatment, same geometry) -- and immediately showed 16 SIMULTANEOUS
! NEGATIVE EIGENVALUES, reproducing closely across two independently
! computed Hessians at NSERCH=2 and NSERCH=3. This ruled out the
! "wrong level of theory generated this geometry" explanation: the geometry
! is now confirmed correct for this functional/solvent combination, and the
! instability appeared purely from enlarging the basis set.
!
! Leading hypothesis: basis-set superposition / basis-flexibility effects.
! def2-TZVPP is a much larger, more diffuse, more polarized basis than
! 6-31G(d,p), and larger/more diffuse basis sets are well known to cause
! exactly this kind of problem specifically for WEAKLY-BOUND, MULTI-FRAGMENT
! complexes (this is 3 separate molecules held together only by H-bonds):
! basis functions centred on one fragment can "borrow" density from a
! neighbouring fragment once the basis is flexible/diffuse enough, creating
! spurious extra soft/unstable directions that are an artefact of basis
! completeness rather than real chemistry.
!
! ============================================================
! What this file does
! ============================================================
! Tests the middle ground: def2-SVP (same Karlsruhe/def2 family as the
! final target def2-TZVPP, but much smaller/less diffuse -- roughly
! comparable in size to 6-31G(d,p)) at the SAME confirmed-good geometry.
!
! If def2-SVP behaves cleanly (few/no negative eigenvalues, similar to
! 6-31G(d,p)): supports the basis-completeness/diffuseness hypothesis, and
! the practical path forward is a staged basis escalation (SVP -> TZVPP)
! rather than jumping straight to the largest basis.
!
! If def2-SVP ALSO shows many negative eigenvalues despite being a small
! basis: the instability is tied to the def2 family's specific functions
! (contraction/polarization scheme) rather than sheer size/diffuseness --
! a different and more surprising finding, worth stopping to think about
! (and a good candidate to post to the GAMESS Google Group either way).
!
! Optimizer settings unchanged from TZVPPd/e (conservative DXMAX=0.01,
! IHREP=2, METHOD=SEMINUM, Cartesian coordinates, NOSYM=1, DIRSCF=.TRUE.)
! since these reflect facts about wB97X-D itself (no analytic Hessian),
! not about basis size.
!
! ------------------------------------------------------------
! def2-SVP basis (Basis Set Exchange, GAMESS-US format) included below,
! converted into the BASNAM block style used throughout this project
! (matching caa005bIRCCML1stdef2TZVPPe.inp's $hbas/$cbas/$obas/$clbas).
! ------------------------------------------------------------

 $CONTRL SCFTYP=RHF RUNTYP=OPTIMIZE DFTTYP=wB97X-D
         COORD=UNIQUE MAXIT=200 NOSYM=1
 $END
 $GUESS GUESS=HUCKEL $END
 $BASIS BASNAM(1)=svpclbas,svpcbas,svpcbas,svpobas,svphbas,svphbas,
          svpobas,svpobas,svphbas,svphbas,svphbas,svphbas,svphbas $END
 $SCF CONV=1.0D-6
     SOSCF=.T.
     DAMP=.F.
     SHIFT=.T.
     DIRSCF=.TRUE.
 $END
 $STATPT OPTTOL=0.0001 NSTEP=300 IHREP=2 HSSEND=.T. DXMAX=0.01 NPRT=1 $END
 $ZMAT DLC=.F. AUTO=.F. $END
 $FORCE METHOD=SEMINUM PROJCT=.T. $END
 $PCM SOLVNT=WATER $END
 $SYSTEM MWORDS=100 MEMDDI=200 $END

 $DATA
Chloroacetaldehyde hydrate and water -- def2-SVP intermediate basis test
C1
Cl   17.0   3.4062263771   0.2091279057   0.2533561081
C     6.0   2.2845933847   0.8634093872  -0.9829615815
